/* Level 16: Stories and true facts (about Grade 4 to 5). Fiction and nonfiction, cause and effect. */
S(16, "The Monsoon", `Every year, between late June and September, something dramatic happens across South Asia. After months of scorching heat, dark clouds roll in from the ocean, and the rain begins. This is the monsoon, and it affects the lives of more than a billion people.

The word "monsoon" comes from the Arabic word mausim, meaning "season." It describes a wind that changes direction with the seasons. During winter, cool, dry winds blow from the land toward the sea. But in summer, the land heats up much faster than the ocean. Hot air over the land rises, and this creates an area of low pressure. Cooler, moist air from the Indian Ocean rushes in to fill the gap. As this damp air moves over the land and is pushed up by hills and mountains, it cools, and the moisture falls as rain.

For farmers in Pakistan, the monsoon is essential. Crops such as rice, cotton and sugarcane depend on its water. Rivers swell, wells refill, and the parched brown land turns green almost overnight. A good monsoon can mean a successful harvest and enough food for the year.

However, the monsoon can also be destructive. When the rains are unusually heavy, rivers burst their banks, causing floods that wash away homes, roads and fields. In 2022, devastating floods covered roughly a third of Pakistan, affecting more than thirty million people. Scientists believe that climate change is making extreme rainfall more frequent, because warmer air can hold more moisture.

In other years, the monsoon arrives late or brings too little rain. This causes drought, when crops wither and water becomes scarce.

Because the monsoon is so important, meteorologists study it carefully. They use satellites, weather balloons and computer models to predict when it will arrive and how much rain it will bring. These forecasts help farmers decide when to plant and help governments prepare for floods.

For people across the region, the first monsoon rain is a moment of celebration. Children run outside to dance in the downpour, the air fills with the earthy smell of wet soil, and everyone breathes a sigh of relief that the long, burning summer is finally over.`, "monsoon, mausim, pressure, moisture, essential, devastating, meteorologists, satellites",
"Why does the land heat up and pull in moist air from the ocean?|In summer the land heats faster than the ocean, hot air rises and cooler moist air rushes in;Why might climate change make floods worse?|Warmer air can hold more moisture, so rainfall becomes more extreme;What do meteorologists do?|Study the weather and predict it");

S(16, "The Angel of Karachi", `In 1951, a young man named Abdul Sattar Edhi opened a small medical dispensary in Karachi with just a few hundred rupees. He had grown up poor, and as a boy he had spent years caring for his mother, who was paralysed. The experience taught him how much suffering existed and how little help was available for the poor.

Edhi lived simply. He owned only two sets of clothes and slept in a small room next to his office. Every rupee that people donated went to helping others. He begged on street corners for donations, and gradually, ordinary people began to trust him.

Over the following decades, his small dispensary grew into the Edhi Foundation, which became one of the largest volunteer ambulance networks in the world. Its ambulances raced through the streets of Pakistan's cities, carrying the injured to hospital, regardless of who they were or whether they could pay. The foundation also ran orphanages, homes for the elderly, shelters for women, and kitchens that fed the hungry.

One of Edhi's most famous initiatives was the cradle. Outside his centres, he placed small cradles with a sign that said, "Do not kill. Leave the baby in the cradle." Thousands of abandoned babies were saved this way and raised in Edhi homes.

Edhi worked alongside his wife, Bilquis, a trained nurse, who became known as the "Mother of Pakistan." Together, they cared for tens of thousands of children.

Edhi refused to accept money from governments, because he wanted to remain independent. He believed that helping others was a duty that belonged to everyone, not just to the rich or powerful. "No religion is higher than humanity," he once said.

When Edhi died in 2016, at the age of eighty-eight, he was given a state funeral. Thousands of people lined the streets. Many of them had been helped by his ambulances, his kitchens or his shelters. Even in death, he continued to give: he had donated his eyes so that others might see.

Today, the Edhi Foundation continues his work. His life is a reminder that one determined person, with very little money but a great deal of compassion, can change millions of lives.`, "Edhi, dispensary, paralysed, donated, initiatives, orphanages, independent, compassion",
"How did Edhi's experience as a boy shape his work?|Caring for his paralysed mother showed him how little help the poor had;Why did Edhi refuse money from governments?|He wanted to stay independent;What does compassion mean?|Caring about other people's suffering and wanting to help");

S(16, "Mohenjo-daro: City of the Ancient Indus", `More than four thousand five hundred years ago, long before the pyramids of Egypt were finished, a remarkable city stood on the banks of the Indus River in what is now Sindh. Today we call it Mohenjo-daro, which means "Mound of the Dead." But in its time, it was a busy, thriving city of perhaps forty thousand people.

Mohenjo-daro was part of the Indus Valley Civilisation, one of the world's earliest great civilisations. What makes it extraordinary is how carefully it was planned. Its streets were laid out in a neat grid, running north to south and east to west. The houses were built from baked bricks of identical size, which suggests that people followed shared rules about building.

Perhaps the most impressive feature was the drainage system. Almost every house had a bathroom and a toilet connected to covered drains that ran beneath the streets. Wastewater flowed away from homes through these channels. Many cities in Europe did not have systems this advanced until thousands of years later.

At the centre of the city stood a large public pool, now called the Great Bath. It was carefully waterproofed with tar and may have been used for religious ceremonies.

Archaeologists have also found toys, jewellery, pottery, and small stone seals carved with animals and a mysterious script. Nobody has ever been able to read this writing. Scholars have tried for a hundred years, but the language of the Indus people remains one of history's great unsolved puzzles.

Interestingly, archaeologists have found very few weapons in Mohenjo-daro and no grand palaces or royal tombs. Some experts believe this means the society may have been relatively peaceful and equal, though nobody knows for certain.

Around 1900 BCE, the city was gradually abandoned. Historians are still not sure why. Possible reasons include changes in the course of the river, long droughts, or a decline in trade.

Mohenjo-daro was rediscovered in the 1920s and is now a UNESCO World Heritage Site. Visitors can walk along its ancient streets and imagine the lives of the people who built one of the first planned cities on Earth.`, "Mohenjo-daro, civilisation, identical, drainage, waterproofed, archaeologists, script, abandoned",
"What made Mohenjo-daro's planning so impressive?|Neat grid streets, same-size bricks and covered drains under the streets;Why do some experts think the city may have been peaceful?|Very few weapons and no grand palaces or royal tombs were found;What does abandoned mean here?|People left the city and stopped living there");

S(16, "The Cotton Harvest", `When the cotton in the fields around her village turned white and fluffy, it meant one thing for Shabana: no school for six weeks. Her family, like most in the area, worked as cotton pickers. Every pair of hands was needed.

At dawn, Shabana followed her mother and aunts into the fields with a long cloth tied around her waist. She plucked the soft cotton bolls from their dry, prickly shells and stuffed them into the cloth. By noon, her fingers were scratched and bleeding, and the sun pressed down like a heavy hand. The women were paid by weight, so the more they picked, the more they earned.

Shabana was eleven and loved school more than anything. Every year, when she returned after the harvest, she had fallen behind. Her teacher was patient, but Shabana felt the gap widening.

That year, she had an idea. She asked her teacher for the lessons she would miss and carried them into the fields in a plastic bag. During the short breaks, while the other women rested, she read. In the evenings, by the light of a kerosene lamp, she worked through sums with her little brother.

Her mother noticed. One night, she sat beside Shabana and watched her work for a long time. "When I was your age," she said quietly, "I wanted to be a teacher. But there was no school in our village then."

Shabana looked up in surprise. Her mother had never told her this.

The next day, her mother spoke to the other women. They agreed to share Shabana's picking among them for one hour each afternoon, so she could study properly in the shade. They also asked the landowner to pay a little extra so the older girls could attend school during the harvest. After much negotiation, he agreed to let them work only in the mornings.

That year, Shabana returned to school without falling behind. Six years later, she became the first girl from her village to attend college. She studied education.

Today, she is a teacher at the village school, and every year at cotton harvest time, she organises evening lessons for the children who work in the fields.`, "bolls, prickly, harvest, widening, kerosene, landowner, negotiation, organises",
"Why did Shabana miss school every year?|She worked as a cotton picker during the six-week harvest;What changed after Shabana's mother saw her studying?|The women shared her work so she could study, and the landowner let girls work only in the mornings;What does negotiation mean?|Talking to reach an agreement");

S(16, "How Paper Is Made", `Paper is so common that we rarely stop to think about it. We write on it, read from it, wrap things in it and even clean with it. But making paper is a fascinating process that combines nature, chemistry and engineering.

Most paper today is made from wood. The trees used are usually fast-growing species such as pine, eucalyptus and spruce. After the trees are cut down, the logs are transported to a paper mill, where their bark is stripped away.

Next, the wood must be broken down into tiny fibres. This can be done in two ways. In mechanical pulping, the wood is ground against a rough stone or between metal discs. In chemical pulping, the wood chips are cooked in a mixture of water and chemicals that dissolve lignin, the natural glue that holds wood fibres together. Both methods produce a thick, wet mush called pulp.

The pulp is then washed and often bleached to make it white. If coloured paper is needed, dyes are added at this stage.

Now comes the clever part. The watery pulp is sprayed onto a huge, fast-moving wire mesh screen. As the water drains through the holes, the fibres tangle together and form a thin, flat mat. This mat passes between heavy rollers that squeeze out more water, then over heated cylinders that dry it completely. Finally, it is wound onto enormous rolls, some weighing several tonnes, and cut into sheets.

Long before paper mills existed, people made paper by hand. The process was invented in China around two thousand years ago, using mulberry bark, hemp and old rags. The knowledge spread along trade routes to the Islamic world, where cities such as Baghdad and Samarkand became famous for paper making. From there, it travelled to Europe.

Because paper uses so many trees, recycling is important. Used paper can be soaked, mashed back into pulp, cleaned of ink, and turned into new paper. Recycling one tonne of paper can save around seventeen trees and a great deal of water and energy. However, fibres become shorter each time they are recycled, so paper can only be recycled five to seven times before the fibres become too weak.

The next time you write in your notebook, remember the long journey it took to get there.`, "fibres, pulp, mechanical, chemical, lignin, bleached, cylinders, recycling",
"What is lignin?|The natural glue that holds wood fibres together;Why can paper only be recycled five to seven times?|The fibres get shorter each time until they are too weak;What does pulp mean in this text?|A thick, wet mush made of broken-down wood fibres");

S(16, "The Snow Leopard Camera", `High in the mountains of Chitral, where the air is thin and cold and the slopes are covered in rock and snow, lives one of the world's most elusive animals: the snow leopard. Its thick grey coat, spotted with dark rosettes, blends so perfectly with the rocky landscape that it is almost invisible. Even people who live in these mountains their entire lives may never see one.

Tariq was fourteen and lived in a village at the edge of a national park. His family kept sheep and goats, and every few years, a snow leopard would raid their pen and kill several animals. When this happened, his father and the other herders were furious. In the past, some had set traps or poisoned carcasses to kill the leopards.

But one autumn, a team of scientists arrived in the village. They were studying snow leopards and wanted to install hidden cameras on the mountain to count how many lived in the area. They needed a local guide who knew the paths. Tariq volunteered.

Over the next few months, he helped the scientists hike to remote ridges and strap motion-activated cameras to rocks. The cameras took a photograph whenever something moved in front of them.

When the scientists retrieved the memory cards in spring, Tariq was astonished. The cameras had captured images of seven different snow leopards, including a mother with two cubs. The scientists explained that each leopard has a unique pattern of spots, like a fingerprint, so they could tell them apart.

They also explained something important. Snow leopards usually hunt wild goats called ibex. When ibex are scarce, because of hunting by humans, the leopards turn to livestock instead. Protecting the ibex would protect the herders' animals too.

The scientists helped the village build stronger, predator-proof pens with wire mesh roofs. They also set up a programme that compensated herders when they lost animals, so they wouldn't need to kill leopards.

The attacks decreased dramatically. And Tariq, who had once thought of snow leopards as enemies, now proudly showed visitors his favourite photograph: the mother leopard on a snowy ridge, her two cubs pressed against her side.`, "Chitral, elusive, rosettes, carcasses, motion-activated, ibex, predator, compensated",
"Why did snow leopards attack the village livestock?|When wild ibex were scarce because of hunting, the leopards turned to livestock;How did the village solve the problem?|They built predator-proof pens and herders were paid when they lost animals;What does elusive mean?|Very hard to find or see");

S(16, "Salt from the Mountain", `Nearly everyone in the world eats salt every day. But few people know that one of the largest salt mines on Earth is in Pakistan. The Khewra Salt Mine, in the Salt Range of Punjab, has been producing salt for hundreds of years, and it contains enough to supply the world for centuries to come.

The salt at Khewra formed around six hundred million years ago. At that time, the area was covered by a shallow sea. As the water slowly evaporated in the hot climate, it left behind thick layers of salt. Over millions of years, these layers were buried under rock and squeezed upward as the land shifted, forming the hills we see today.

According to local legend, the salt was discovered by accident around 320 BCE. The story says that the horses of Alexander the Great's army stopped to rest in the area and began licking the rocks. When soldiers noticed this, they tasted the rocks themselves and discovered they were made of salt.

Mining at Khewra became organised in the nineteenth century, when British engineers designed a system of tunnels. Today, the mine stretches about forty kilometres into the mountain and has nineteen levels. Miners use a technique called room-and-pillar mining: they remove half of the salt and leave the other half standing as enormous pillars to support the ceiling.

Inside the mine, the salt is not just white. It comes in shades of pink, red and orange, caused by tiny amounts of minerals such as iron. This pink salt has become popular around the world as "Himalayan salt."

The mine has also become a tourist attraction. Visitors can ride a small train deep into the mountain and walk through glowing caverns. Workers have carved a miniature mosque from salt bricks, lit from within so that the walls glow like amber. There is even a clinic inside the mine, because some people believe the salty air helps with breathing problems such as asthma.

Khewra's salt is used not only for cooking, but also in industry, for making glass, soap and chemicals. In winter, it is spread on icy roads to melt the ice and make them safer. A simple substance from an ancient sea still plays an important role in modern life.`, "Khewra, evaporated, legend, Alexander, organised, pillars, minerals, asthma",
"How did the salt at Khewra form?|An ancient sea evaporated and left layers of salt that were buried and pushed up;Why do miners leave half the salt standing as pillars?|To support the ceiling so the mine doesn't collapse;What does evaporated mean?|Turned from water into vapour and disappeared into the air");

S(16, "The Science Fair Disaster", `For two months, Hamza and his friend Bilal had worked on their science fair project: a model volcano that would erupt with foaming red lava. They had built it out of wire, newspaper and plaster, painted it brown and grey, and even added tiny trees made of sponge. Inside the crater was a hidden plastic bottle.

The secret ingredient was a chemical reaction. When baking soda, a base, is mixed with vinegar, an acid, they react to produce carbon dioxide gas. The gas creates bubbles that make the mixture foam up and overflow. Add red food colouring and a squirt of washing-up liquid, and the result looks like lava.

They had tested it three times at home. It worked perfectly every time.

On the day of the fair, the hall was crowded with students, parents and teachers. The judges came round to their table. Hamza explained the science proudly. Then Bilal poured vinegar into the bottle.

Nothing happened.

They stared at it. Bilal added more vinegar. A few tired bubbles appeared, then nothing. The judges glanced at each other and made notes on their clipboards. Someone at the next table giggled.

Hamza's face burned. Then he noticed something. The baking soda in the bottle was clumped together in a hard, damp lump.

"It got wet," he said slowly. "We put the baking soda in this morning, and the plaster was still a bit damp. The moisture must have started the reaction early, and used it up."

One of the judges, an elderly science teacher, leaned forward with interest. "So what would you do differently?"

Hamza thought carefully. "Keep the baking soda completely dry until the last second. Maybe in a sealed packet. And test the plaster first to make sure it's dry."

The judge nodded. Then she asked him to explain exactly why water affected the baking soda. Hamza explained, and then explained how they could test their theory.

Later, when the winners were announced, Hamza and Bilal didn't win first prize. But they received a special award for "Best Scientific Thinking." The note from the judges said, "Real science is about understanding why things go wrong."

That weekend, they tried again with dry baking soda. The volcano erupted spectacularly, splashing red foam all over Bilal's mother's courtyard.`, "erupt, plaster, crater, reaction, carbon dioxide, moisture, theory, spectacularly",
"Why didn't the volcano erupt at the fair?|The baking soda got damp from the plaster and reacted too early;Why did they win the award for Best Scientific Thinking?|They figured out why it went wrong and how to test their idea;What gas is made when baking soda and vinegar mix?|Carbon dioxide");

S(16, "Birds That Fly Across Continents", `Every autumn, as winter approaches in Siberia and Central Asia, millions of birds begin one of nature's most astonishing journeys. They fly thousands of kilometres south to warmer lands, including Pakistan, where they spend the winter before returning north in spring. This seasonal journey is called migration.

One of the most important migration routes in the world passes directly over Pakistan. It is known as the Indus Flyway, or Green Route Number 4. Every year, birds such as cranes, ducks, geese, flamingos and falcons travel along it. Some fly as high as eight kilometres, crossing the mountains of the Hindu Kush and the Himalayas.

Why do birds migrate? The main reason is food. In the far north, winter brings snow and ice that cover the plants, insects and water that birds depend on. By flying south, they find lakes, wetlands and fields where food is still available. In spring, they return north, where long summer days provide plenty of insects and space to raise their chicks.

Scientists are still discovering exactly how birds find their way. They seem to use several methods at once. Many birds navigate by the position of the sun during the day and the stars at night. Some can sense the Earth's magnetic field, almost like an internal compass. Others recognise landmarks such as rivers, coastlines and mountain ranges. Young birds often learn the route by following older, experienced members of their flock.

Pakistan has many important wetlands where these birds stop to rest and feed. Lakes such as Haleji in Sindh and Uchhali in Punjab attract tens of thousands of birds each winter.

Sadly, migrating birds face many dangers. Illegal hunting, especially of falcons and houbara bustards, has reduced their numbers. Wetlands are being drained for farming or polluted by waste. Power lines can also be deadly for large birds such as cranes.

Conservation groups are working to protect wetlands and educate communities about the importance of these travellers. Some villages that once hunted cranes now protect them and welcome birdwatchers instead. When you see a V-shaped flock of birds in the winter sky, remember that they may have flown all the way from Siberia, guided by the sun, the stars and the Earth itself.`, "Siberia, migration, flyway, wetlands, navigate, magnetic, landmarks, conservation",
"Why do birds fly south in autumn?|To find food, because snow and ice cover their food in the north;Name two ways birds find their way.|The sun and stars, the Earth's magnetic field, or landmarks like rivers and mountains;What does migration mean?|A seasonal journey from one place to another and back");

S(16, "The Water Filter", `In Laila's village, water came from a hand pump in the centre of the lane. It looked clear, but every summer, children fell sick with stomach illnesses. Her little brother had been sick three times in one year.

Laila was thirteen and loved science. When her teacher explained that water could look clean but still contain invisible bacteria and tiny particles of dirt, she started thinking. If the water could be cleaned before people drank it, perhaps fewer children would become ill.

She researched water filters in the school library and learned that layers of sand, gravel and charcoal could trap dirt and some harmful substances. She decided to build one.

She cut the bottom off a large plastic bottle and turned it upside down. Inside, she placed layers: a piece of cotton cloth at the narrow neck, then a layer of crushed charcoal from the kitchen stove, then fine sand, then coarse sand, and finally small pebbles on top. Each layer had a purpose. The pebbles caught leaves and large particles. The sand trapped smaller dirt. The charcoal absorbed some chemicals and improved the taste.

She poured muddy water from the canal into the top. Slowly, drop by drop, much clearer water emerged from the bottom.

Laila was thrilled, but her teacher cautioned her. "Your filter removes dirt," she said, "but it may not remove all bacteria. Germs are so small that some can slip through the sand."

Laila was disappointed, but she kept researching. She discovered that sunlight could help. If clear water is placed in clean plastic bottles and left in strong sunshine for six hours, the ultraviolet rays in the sunlight can kill many harmful germs. This method is called solar water disinfection.

She combined both methods: first filtering the water to make it clear, then placing it in bottles on the roof in the sun. She showed her family and neighbours how to do it.

The following summer, far fewer children in the lane became sick. Laila's brother wasn't ill once.

Her project won a prize at the district science fair. But Laila was proudest of something else: the row of shining water bottles that now sat on almost every roof in her lane.`, "bacteria, particles, charcoal, absorbed, cautioned, ultraviolet, disinfection, emerged",
"What does each layer of Laila's filter do?|Pebbles catch big pieces, sand traps smaller dirt, and charcoal absorbs some chemicals;Why did Laila also use sunlight?|The filter might not remove all germs, but ultraviolet rays in sunlight can kill them;What does bacteria mean?|Tiny living things, too small to see, that can cause illness");

S(16, "The Highest Mountains on Earth", `Pakistan is home to some of the highest mountains in the world. Five of the fourteen peaks on Earth that rise above eight thousand metres are found in the country's north, in the Karakoram and Himalaya ranges. The most famous of these is K2.

At 8,611 metres, K2 is the second-highest mountain on Earth, only about two hundred metres lower than Mount Everest. But many climbers consider it far more difficult and dangerous. Its slopes are steeper, its weather is more unpredictable, and avalanches are common. For many years, roughly one in four climbers who reached the summit died on the way down. Climbers sometimes call it "the Savage Mountain."

How did such enormous mountains form? The answer lies deep beneath our feet. The Earth's outer layer is divided into giant pieces called tectonic plates, which move very slowly, only a few centimetres each year, about as fast as your fingernails grow. Around fifty million years ago, the plate carrying India collided with the plate carrying Asia. Neither plate could sink beneath the other, so the land crumpled upward, like a rug pushed against a wall. This collision created the Himalayas and the Karakoram.

Remarkably, the collision is still happening. The Indian plate continues to push northward, and the mountains are still rising by a few millimetres each year. This movement also causes earthquakes, which are common in the region.

The mountains are important for more than their beauty. They contain thousands of glaciers, enormous rivers of ice that move slowly down the valleys. In summer, as the ice melts, it feeds great rivers such as the Indus. Millions of people downstream depend on this water for drinking, farming and electricity.

However, scientists have noticed that many glaciers are shrinking because of rising global temperatures. This could eventually affect the water supply for millions of people. Sudden melting can also cause dangerous floods when lakes formed by glaciers burst their banks.

The mountains are home to hardy communities such as the Balti people, who have lived in these harsh conditions for centuries. Many work as porters and guides for climbing expeditions. Without their skill and strength, few climbers would ever reach the summits.`, "Karakoram, unpredictable, avalanches, summit, tectonic, collided, glaciers, expeditions",
"How were the Himalayas and Karakoram formed?|The Indian plate collided with the Asian plate and the land crumpled upward;Why are the glaciers important to people far away?|Their melting ice feeds rivers like the Indus that millions depend on;What does collided mean?|Crashed into each other");

S(16, "Grandfather's Pigeons", `On the flat roof of their house in the old city, Faraz's grandfather kept forty pigeons in a large wooden coop. Every evening, he opened the door and let them fly. They wheeled in great circles above the rooftops, their wings flashing grey and white in the setting sun. Then, at a whistle, they returned home.

"How do they always find their way back?" Faraz asked one evening.

Grandfather smiled. "Pigeons have a gift for finding home. In the past, people used them to carry messages hundreds of kilometres." He told Faraz that scientists believe pigeons navigate partly by sensing the Earth's magnetic field and partly by recognising landmarks such as roads and rivers. Some studies suggest they may even use smell.

Faraz was fascinated. Over the following months, he helped Grandfather feed the birds, clean the coop and care for the chicks. He learned to recognise each pigeon by its markings. His favourite was a small white bird with a grey ring around its neck. He named her Chandni, which means moonlight.

One day, a powerful dust storm swept across the city just as the pigeons were flying. The sky turned brown, and the wind howled. Grandfather whistled again and again, and one by one, the birds struggled back to the coop. All except Chandni.

Faraz waited on the roof until dark. She didn't return. That night, he couldn't sleep. The next day, there was still no sign of her. Grandfather put a hand on his shoulder. "Sometimes in a storm they are blown far away," he said gently. "If she is alive, she will try to come home."

Faraz checked the roof every morning and every evening. Three days passed. Then four. He began to lose hope.

On the sixth morning, as he climbed the stairs, he heard a soft cooing. Sitting on the edge of the coop, dusty and thin, with one ruffled wing, was Chandni.

Faraz cried out with joy. Grandfather examined her carefully. "She has flown a long way," he said. "Look at how worn her feathers are. She may have been blown fifty kilometres or more."

Faraz fed her by hand that day. And he understood, in a new way, what his grandfather meant about the gift of finding home.`, "coop, wheeled, navigate, magnetic, landmarks, fascinated, Chandni, examined",
"How might pigeons find their way home?|By sensing the Earth's magnetic field, recognising landmarks, and maybe smell;Why didn't Chandni come back with the others?|The dust storm probably blew her far away;What does navigate mean?|Find your way from one place to another");

S(16, "How Our Hearts Work", `Place your hand on the left side of your chest. You will feel a steady thump, thump, thump. That is your heart, a powerful muscle about the size of your fist, working every second of every day to keep you alive.

The heart's job is to pump blood around your body. Blood carries oxygen and nutrients to every cell, and it collects waste products such as carbon dioxide. Without a constant supply of oxygen, your brain and other organs could not survive for more than a few minutes.

The heart has four chambers. The two upper chambers are called atria, and the two lower chambers are called ventricles. The right side of the heart receives blood that has travelled around the body and has used up its oxygen. It pumps this blood to the lungs. In the lungs, the blood releases carbon dioxide, which you breathe out, and picks up fresh oxygen from the air you breathe in.

This oxygen-rich blood then returns to the left side of the heart. The left ventricle, the strongest chamber, pumps it out through a large artery called the aorta to the rest of the body. Arteries carry blood away from the heart, and veins carry it back.

To keep blood flowing in the right direction, the heart has four valves, small flaps that open and close with each beat. The "lub-dub" sound a doctor hears through a stethoscope is the sound of these valves snapping shut.

Your heart beats about seventy to one hundred times per minute when you are resting. That adds up to roughly one hundred thousand beats every day, and nearly three billion beats in an average lifetime. When you exercise, your muscles need more oxygen, so your heart beats faster to deliver it.

Like any muscle, the heart becomes stronger with exercise. Activities such as running, swimming, cycling and playing football help keep it healthy. Eating plenty of fruit, vegetables and whole grains, and avoiding too much salt, sugar and fried food, also helps. Smoking is one of the most harmful things for the heart.

Doctors measure how well the heart is working by checking a person's pulse, the beat of blood through the arteries. You can find your own pulse on the inside of your wrist or on the side of your neck. Try counting the beats for fifteen seconds, then multiply by four. That's your heart rate.`, "oxygen, nutrients, chambers, atria, ventricles, aorta, valves, stethoscope",
"Why does blood go to the lungs?|To get rid of carbon dioxide and pick up fresh oxygen;Why does your heart beat faster when you exercise?|Your muscles need more oxygen, so the heart pumps faster to deliver it;What do valves do in the heart?|They open and close to keep blood flowing the right way");

S(16, "The Cricket Bat Maker", `In the city of Sialkot, there is a workshop that has been making cricket bats for three generations. The smell of freshly cut willow fills the air, and the floor is always covered in curls of pale wood shavings.

Usman was fifteen and worked there after school with his uncle. He loved cricket, but he had never thought about how a bat was made until he started working in the shop.

The best bats, his uncle explained, are made from English willow, a wood that is both light and incredibly strong. Its fibres compress when the ball strikes, then spring back, which gives the shot its power. Cheaper bats are made from Kashmir willow, which is heavier and less springy.

The process begins with a block of wood called a cleft. It is dried slowly for many months, sometimes over a year, so that it doesn't crack or warp. Then craftsmen shape it by hand, using drawknives and planes to carve the distinctive curved back and flat face of the bat.

Next comes a crucial step called pressing. The face of the bat is squeezed under enormous pressure in a machine. This compresses the fibres and hardens the surface so it can withstand the impact of a hard cricket ball moving at over one hundred and forty kilometres per hour. If a bat is not pressed properly, it may crack on the first strong hit.

The handle is made separately from cane, layered with thin strips of rubber to absorb vibration. It is glued into a V-shaped slot cut into the top of the blade, called the splice. Finally, the bat is sanded smooth, oiled with linseed oil to protect the wood, and fitted with a rubber grip.

One day, a famous Pakistani cricketer visited the workshop to order a bat. Usman watched as his uncle selected a cleft, held it up to the light and tapped it with his knuckles, listening carefully. "You can hear the quality," he told Usman. "Good willow rings like a bell."

Usman spent the next two weeks helping to shape that bat. When the cricketer collected it, he swung it a few times and smiled. A month later, Usman watched on television as he hit a century with it. Usman had never felt prouder of anything in his life.`, "willow, fibres, compress, cleft, drawknives, withstand, vibration, linseed",
"Why is English willow used for the best bats?|It is light and strong, and its fibres spring back to give shots power;Why is the bat face pressed?|To harden the surface so it doesn't crack when hit by a fast ball;What does withstand mean?|To stand up against something without breaking");

S(16, "Sunlight Into Electricity", `In many villages in Pakistan, electricity is unreliable. Power cuts, sometimes called load-shedding, can last for hours every day, especially in summer. Families sit in the dark, fans stop working in the extreme heat, and children struggle to do their homework. But an increasing number of homes have found a solution on their roofs: solar panels.

Solar panels turn sunlight directly into electricity. Each panel is made up of many small units called solar cells, usually made from a material called silicon. Silicon is found in ordinary sand, but for solar cells it must be purified and processed in factories.

When sunlight strikes a solar cell, tiny particles of light called photons knock electrons loose from the silicon atoms. The cell is designed so that these freed electrons flow in one direction, creating an electric current. Wires collect this current and carry it to where it is needed.

The electricity produced by solar panels is direct current, or DC. Most household appliances use alternating current, or AC. So solar systems usually include a device called an inverter, which converts DC into AC. Many systems also include batteries that store extra electricity produced during the day, so it can be used at night.

Pakistan is an excellent place for solar power. Most parts of the country receive strong sunshine for more than three hundred days a year. In recent years, the price of solar panels has fallen dramatically, making them affordable for many more families and businesses.

Solar power has important benefits. Unlike electricity from coal or oil, it doesn't produce smoke or the greenhouse gases that cause climate change. Once the panels are installed, the sunlight is free.

However, there are challenges. Solar panels only produce electricity when the sun is shining, so output drops on cloudy days and stops completely at night. Batteries can solve this problem, but they are expensive. Panels also need to be kept clean, because dust can block the sunlight and reduce their efficiency.

In villages that were never connected to the electricity grid, small solar systems have changed lives. A single panel can power lights, a fan and a phone charger. Children can study in the evenings, health clinics can store medicines in refrigerators, and small shops can stay open after dark.`, "load-shedding, silicon, photons, electrons, current, inverter, greenhouse, efficiency",
"What happens when sunlight hits a solar cell?|Photons knock electrons loose, and they flow to make an electric current;Why do many solar systems need batteries?|Panels don't make electricity at night, so batteries store extra power from the day;What does an inverter do?|Changes direct current into alternating current for home appliances");

S(16, "The Two Shopkeepers", `On the same street in Quetta, two men opened shops selling dried fruit and nuts on the same day. Their names were Rahim and Naseer.

Rahim was ambitious. He wanted to become rich quickly. He bought the cheapest almonds, walnuts and apricots he could find, mixed old stock with new, and sometimes added a few stones to the bottom of his sacks to make them heavier on the scale. His prices were the lowest on the street, and at first, his shop was always crowded.

Naseer took a different approach. He travelled to the orchards in the valleys himself and bought only the finest fruit and nuts directly from farmers. He paid them fairly, even when he could have bargained for less. His prices were higher than Rahim's, and in the first few months, he had far fewer customers.

"You're a fool," Rahim told him. "People want cheap prices, not perfect apricots."

Naseer just shrugged. "We'll see."

Over time, things began to change. Customers who bought from Rahim discovered stones in their bags or found that the walnuts tasted stale and bitter. They complained to their friends and stopped coming back. Rahim had to keep lowering his prices to attract new customers, which meant he earned less and less.

Meanwhile, Naseer's customers were delighted. His almonds were crisp, his apricots sweet and soft. They told their families and neighbours. Hotel owners and restaurant cooks began ordering from him in large quantities. Even shopkeepers from other cities started buying his products to sell in their own shops.

The farmers who supplied Naseer were also loyal. When there was a poor harvest and nuts were scarce, they sold their best produce to him first, because he had always treated them fairly.

Five years later, Naseer had expanded into three shops and was exporting dried fruit to Dubai. Rahim's shop had closed.

One evening, Rahim visited Naseer, embarrassed, and asked for a job. Naseer could have refused, but he didn't. He hired Rahim to manage his storeroom, on one condition: "In my business," he said, "the scale is always honest."

Rahim worked for Naseer for many years. He learned that trust, once earned, grows like interest on money, and that cheating is a debt that eventually must be paid.`, "Quetta, ambitious, stale, approach, bargained, quantities, exporting, condition",
"How did Rahim cheat his customers?|He mixed old stock with new and put stones in sacks to make them heavier;Why did Naseer's business grow?|Customers trusted his good quality and told others, and farmers stayed loyal because he paid them fairly;What does ambitious mean?|Wanting very much to succeed, become rich or important");

S(16, "Why Do Leaves Change Colour?", `In the northern valleys of Pakistan, such as Swat, Hunza and Kalash, autumn transforms the landscape. Hillsides that were green all summer suddenly blaze with yellow, orange and red. Apricot trees, poplars and maples change colour, creating one of the most beautiful sights of the year. But why does this happen?

The answer lies in the chemicals inside leaves. During spring and summer, leaves are green because they contain a pigment called chlorophyll. Chlorophyll is essential for photosynthesis, the process by which plants use sunlight, water and carbon dioxide to make sugar for food. Leaves contain so much chlorophyll in summer that its green colour hides other pigments that are also present.

As autumn arrives, the days become shorter and the temperatures cooler. Trees respond to these changes by preparing for winter. In cold weather, especially when the ground freezes, it becomes difficult for trees to get enough water. Thin, delicate leaves would lose too much water and be damaged by frost. So many trees, called deciduous trees, shed their leaves and rest through the winter.

Before letting their leaves fall, trees stop producing chlorophyll. The green colour gradually breaks down and fades. As it disappears, other pigments that were hidden all summer become visible. Carotenoids, the same pigments that make carrots orange and bananas yellow, produce yellow and orange colours.

Red and purple colours come from a different group of pigments called anthocyanins. Unlike carotenoids, these are not present in leaves all summer. Some trees produce them in autumn, especially when there are sunny days and cool nights. Scientists think anthocyanins may protect the leaves from strong sunlight, helping the tree recover as many nutrients as possible before the leaves fall.

Finally, a special layer of cells forms where each leaf stem joins the branch. This layer slowly cuts the leaf off from the tree. Eventually, wind or rain knocks the leaf free, and it flutters to the ground.

Fallen leaves are not wasted. As they decompose, they return nutrients to the soil, which helps the tree grow again in spring. Some people collect fallen leaves to make compost, a natural fertiliser for gardens.

Next time you see autumn leaves, remember that the beautiful colours are a sign of a tree carefully preparing to survive the winter.`, "chlorophyll, pigment, photosynthesis, deciduous, carotenoids, anthocyanins, decompose, compost",
"Why do leaves turn yellow and orange in autumn?|Trees stop making green chlorophyll, so hidden yellow and orange pigments show;Why do deciduous trees drop their leaves in winter?|Thin leaves would lose too much water and be damaged by frost;What does photosynthesis mean?|How plants use sunlight, water and carbon dioxide to make food");

S(16, "The Camel Caravan", `For thousands of years, before trucks and trains, goods were carried across the deserts of Asia and Africa by camel caravans. These long lines of camels, sometimes hundreds of animals, travelled along ancient trade routes, carrying salt, spices, cloth, gold and many other treasures.

Camels are perfectly suited to desert life. Contrary to popular belief, a camel's hump does not store water. It stores fat. When food is scarce, the camel's body can break down this fat for energy. A camel with a full, upright hump is healthy and well fed. One with a drooping hump has been travelling for a long time without enough food.

Camels are remarkably good at conserving water. They can go for a week or more without drinking, even in extreme heat. When they do find water, they can drink over one hundred litres in just a few minutes. Their bodies are designed to lose very little moisture. Their nostrils can close to keep out sand and trap moisture from their breath. Their kidneys are extremely efficient, producing very concentrated urine.

Camels have other adaptations too. Their long eyelashes, sometimes in two rows, protect their eyes from blowing sand. Their wide, padded feet spread out on soft sand, preventing them from sinking. Thick fur on their backs protects them from the burning sun, while thinner fur elsewhere helps them lose heat. They can also tolerate body temperatures that would be dangerous for most mammals, which means they sweat less and save water.

In Pakistan, camels are still important, particularly in the deserts of Cholistan, Thar and Balochistan. Families use them to carry water, firewood and goods across sandy areas where vehicles cannot easily travel. Camel milk is an important part of the diet for many desert communities. It is rich in vitamins and can be stored longer than cow's milk.

Camels also play a role in culture. At festivals in Cholistan, camels are decorated with colourful saddles, bells and embroidered blankets. Camel races and camel dancing contests draw large crowds.

Although trucks have replaced most caravans, camels remain living reminders of a time when the desert was crossed slowly, one patient step at a time, guided by the stars and the knowledge of experienced herders.`, "caravans, contrary, conserving, concentrated, adaptations, tolerate, Cholistan, embroidered",
"What does a camel's hump really store?|Fat, which the camel can use for energy;Name two ways camels are adapted to the desert.|Long eyelashes, wide padded feet, closing nostrils, or saving water;What does adaptations mean?|Special features that help an animal survive where it lives");

S(16, "The Lighthouse Keeper's Daughter", `On a rocky point near the coast of Balochistan stood an old lighthouse. For generations, the Baloch family had kept its light burning to warn fishing boats away from the dangerous rocks hidden beneath the waves.

Sameena was twelve and had grown up in the small stone house beside the tower. Her father was the keeper. Every evening, she helped him climb the spiral staircase, one hundred and twenty-three steps, to clean the great glass lens and check the lamp.

The lens fascinated her. It was made of many curved rings of glass, arranged like the layers of an onion. Her father explained that it was a Fresnel lens, invented in France two hundred years ago. Its rings bent and concentrated the light from a single lamp into a powerful beam that could be seen thirty kilometres out to sea. Without the lens, the light would spread in every direction and fade quickly.

One night in winter, a violent storm struck the coast. Rain hammered against the windows, and huge waves crashed against the rocks below. Then, just after midnight, the power failed. The electric lamp at the top of the tower went dark.

Sameena's father was in town, trapped by flooded roads. She was alone with her mother and baby brother.

Out at sea, Sameena could just make out the faint lights of fishing boats struggling toward the harbour. Without the lighthouse, they would have no way to see the rocks.

She remembered something her father had told her. In the old days, before electricity, the lighthouse had used oil lamps. There was still an emergency kerosene lamp stored in a cupboard in the tower.

Sameena grabbed a torch and ran up all one hundred and twenty-three steps. Her legs burned. At the top, she found the old lamp, filled it with kerosene with shaking hands, and lit it. She placed it carefully at the centre of the great lens.

It wasn't as bright as the electric lamp. But the lens did its work, gathering the small flame and sending a strong beam sweeping across the dark sea.

She stayed at the top all night, keeping the lamp burning. By dawn, every boat was safely in harbour. The fishermen learned what had happened, and that afternoon, a dozen of them climbed the hill to thank the girl who had kept the light.`, "Balochistan, generations, spiral, Fresnel, concentrated, harbour, kerosene, emergency",
"What does the Fresnel lens do?|It bends and concentrates the light into a powerful beam that can be seen far out at sea;Why was it so important to keep the light burning?|Fishing boats needed it to avoid the dangerous rocks;What does concentrated mean here?|Gathered together into one strong beam");

S(16, "Earthquakes: When the Ground Shakes", `On the morning of 8 October 2005, a powerful earthquake struck northern Pakistan and Kashmir. In less than a minute, entire towns were destroyed. More than seventy thousand people lost their lives, and millions were left homeless. It was one of the deadliest natural disasters in the country's history. But what causes the ground beneath our feet to shake so violently?

The Earth's outer layer, called the crust, is broken into enormous slabs called tectonic plates. These plates fit together like pieces of a giant puzzle and float on a layer of hot, partly melted rock beneath them. Although they move extremely slowly, only a few centimetres each year, they carry tremendous force.

Where two plates meet, their edges often get stuck because of friction. Pressure builds up over many years as the plates keep trying to move. Eventually, the rock can no longer withstand the strain. It suddenly breaks and slips, releasing huge amounts of energy in the form of waves that travel through the ground. These are called seismic waves, and they cause the shaking we feel during an earthquake.

Pakistan lies in an area where the Indian plate is pushing northward against the Eurasian plate. This collision is what created the Himalayas, and it also makes northern Pakistan one of the most earthquake-prone regions in the world.

Scientists measure earthquakes using instruments called seismographs, which record the movement of the ground. The strength of an earthquake is expressed as its magnitude. The 2005 earthquake had a magnitude of 7.6. Each step up the scale represents about thirty-two times more energy, so a magnitude 7 earthquake is far more powerful than a magnitude 6.

Unfortunately, scientists cannot yet predict exactly when an earthquake will happen. However, we can reduce the damage. Buildings can be designed to bend rather than collapse, using reinforced concrete and flexible frames. After 2005, Pakistan introduced stricter building rules in earthquake zones.

Knowing what to do can also save lives. During an earthquake, experts recommend: drop to the ground, take cover under a sturdy table, and hold on until the shaking stops. Stay away from windows and heavy objects that might fall. If you are outside, move to an open area away from buildings, trees and power lines.`, "tectonic, friction, withstand, seismic, Eurasian, seismographs, magnitude, reinforced",
"What causes an earthquake?|Pressure builds where plates are stuck, then the rock suddenly breaks and slips, releasing energy;What should you do during an earthquake?|Drop down, take cover under a sturdy table, and hold on;What does magnitude mean?|How strong an earthquake is");
